::ENG3_U06_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: A slide has a full paragraph of text. What revision best improves audience experience? {
~Add another paragraph.#Incorrect. More text increases the reading burden.
=Use a short claim phrase and explain details aloud.#Correct. This supports listening and reduces cognitive load.
~Make the paragraph smaller.#Incorrect. Tiny text is harder to read.
~Add unrelated images around it.#Incorrect. Unrelated visuals distract.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A speaker's slide lists evidence but the oral explanation gives a different claim. What is the problem? {
~The slide has too little decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration is not the issue.
~The evidence is automatically credible.#Incorrect. Credibility is not shown by mismatch.
~The audience will enjoy the surprise.#Incorrect. Mismatch can confuse the audience.
=The digital support does not match the oral focus.#Correct. Slides and speech should work together.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which slide title best supports complex text analysis? {
=Setting Mirrors Character Conflict.#Correct. This title previews an analytical relationship.
~Stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~My Slide.#Incorrect. This does not help the audience.
~Random Pictures.#Incorrect. This does not support analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: Feedback says a slide is visually busy. What revision best responds? {
~Add more fonts and colors.#Incorrect. That increases visual clutter.
~Keep every image because more is better.#Incorrect. More is not always clearer.
=Remove unrelated visuals and keep the one that clarifies the claim.#Correct. This revises for clarity and audience experience.
~Make the source note unreadable.#Incorrect. Source information should support credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which design choice most improves audience experience? {
~Tiny text with a full paragraph.#Incorrect. Tiny text is hard to read.
=A clear title, one relevant visual, and a short evidence phrase.#Correct. This supports understanding without overload.
~Four decorative images and no claim.#Incorrect. Decoration without purpose distracts.
~A blank slide with no information.#Incorrect. The slide should support the presentation.
}
